Context for e67aca8 (T040): The contracts README already stated the rule this task implements: a reference answer that conflicts with an expected outcome is kept and flagged for review, never resolved silently. So the conflict is a finding, not a validation failure: rejecting it would force the dataset author to edit the record before anyone saw the disagreement, and rewriting it silently is exactly what the contract forbids. Two finding kinds cover the two places a disagreement lives: the acceptance meaning of one reference against the expected outcome of its check, and the stated overall outcome against the aggregate of the stated check outcomes (the run-report order with no error and no skip). The implied outcome comes from policy::answer_sets, now shared instead of duplicated, so a reference label and an assessment answer from one meaning and cannot drift; the review marker dominates the answer, because an ambiguous reference routes to one human whatever proposal the record also states. Hard failures reuse existing reason codes (unknown_field for a check the definition does not declare, unknown_label for an answer or level outside the declared labels, invalid_field_type for the wrong question kind or a reference on a rule check); each defect is exactly one of those, and a new code would have required the additive-change sweep of the registry, the README table, and the states rows for no gain. The provenance summary counts only the records that state one expected-label object, because a record without references carries no label provenance to summarize, so the four author-and-review counts sum to labeled; only the reviewed counts are reviewed evidence, which is the invariant summary.reviewed() states directly and the tests hold against one dataset of proposals alone. Finding field paths carry the /records/<line> prefix the way failures do, so one report can point at the exact field from either surface. The label review is data from the core through the binding, never a wrapper count, and fixtures/datasets/labels.json pins it for the later Python SDK with the same runner rule the Rust core and the public boundary both answer.
